      "text": "  \"output_tail\": \"[run] {\\\"ok\\\":true,\\\"action\\\":\\\"read\\\",\\\"page\\\":{\\\"title\\\":\\\"\\\",\\\"url\\\":\\\"https://www.clintware.com/skills/linkedin-evidence-post/SKILL.md\\\",\\\"description\\\":\\\"\\\",\\\"headings\\\":[],\\\"text\\\":\\\"---\\\\nname: linkedin-evidence-post\\\\ndescription: Draft concise, evidence-led LinkedIn posts from a concrete observation, article, field note, metric, or professional lesson. Use for reflective professional posts, article reshares, interview or career lessons, operating insights, and posts meant to demonstrate judgment without sounding scripted, over-produced, or engagement-baited.\\\\n---\\\\n\\\\n# LinkedIn Evidence Post\\\\n\\\\nTurn a real observation into a LinkedIn post that sounds like a person who learned something, not a content template trying to manufacture authority.\\\\n\\\\nThe governing rule is:\\\\n\\\\n> **OBSERVATION -> PROOF -> PRINCIPLE -> RELEVANCE -> STOP**\\\\n\\\\nFor interview or career-reflection posts, a useful compact variant is:\\\\n\\\\n> **PRESENT -> PROOF -> FUTURE -> STOP**\\\\n\\\\nThe final step is intentional. A strong post does not need to answer every possible question. It should make the point clearly, leave one useful thread for the reader to pull, and end.\\\\n\\\\n## Default workflow\\\\n\\\\n1. **Identify the actual point.** Reduce the post to one sentence: what changed in the author's thinking, behavior, or operating method?\\\\n2. **Recover evidence before drafting.** Use supplied notes, metrics, feedback, transcripts, article context, or observed outcomes. Never invent a supporting anecdote or number.\\\\n3. **Choose one proof point.** Prefer one concrete metric, short example, or observed contrast over a stack of claims.\\\\n4. **Extract the principle.** State what the proof changed or clarified. Do not merely retell the event.\\\\n5. **Make the relevance explicit.** Connect the lesson to how the author now works, decides, prepares, leads, builds, or communicates.\\\\n6. **Stop before over-explaining.** Remove the sentence that tries to pre-answer every objection.\\\\n7. **Run the Devil's Advocate gate.** Read the post as a skeptical recruiter, hiring manager, customer, peer, executive, or prospective collaborator.\\\\n8. **Return the finished post in natural paragraphs.** Use isolated one-line text only when a framework, contrast, or closing line benefits from visual emphasis.\\\\n\\\\n## When the post references an article\\\\n\\\\nTreat the article as the spark, not the content.\\\\n\\\\nUse this sequence:\\\\n\\\\n**ARTICLE IDEA -> FIELD OBSERVATION -> PROOF -> OWN FRAMEWORK -> PRACTICAL TAKEAWAY**\\\\n\\\\nThe post should add something the article does not already say. A useful opening may simply establish alignment, but the body must quickly move to the author's own evidence or experience.\\\\n\\\\nDo not:\\\\n- summarize the whole article;\\\\n- praise it for a paragraph before adding a point;\\\\n- copy distinctive wording from the source;\\\\n- present the source's conclusion as personal evidence;\\\\n- force a disagreement merely to sound original.\\\\n\\\\n## The proof-breadcrumb rule\\\\n\\\\nUse enough evidence to establish credibility, then leave room for the next question.\\\\n\\\\nA proof point should behave like a breadcrumb: concrete enough to be believable, but bounded enough that a reader can naturally ask for the next layer.\\\\n\\\\nThis is especially useful when translating interview lessons into public writing. A concise answer or post often works better when it creates the next useful question instead of attempting to close every branch of the conversation.\\\\n\\\\n## Preparation versus performance\\\\n\\\\nWhen the lesson involves communication, interviews, presentations, or executive conversations, distinguish preparation from rehearsal.\\\\n\\\\nThe stronger operating pattern is:\\\\n\\\\n- know the numbers;\\\\n- know the stories;\\\\n- know the audience;\\\\n- know the point;\